-- title:
--   Eqs. (18)–(19) — $Y=(\cos\alpha)(\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf S)+i(\sin\alpha)(\mathbf m\cdot\mathbf S)$, rotated to $Z$
-- statement:
--   Let $S_1,S_2,S_3$ be the spin-1 matrices (13) and $\xi\in\mathbb C^3$ a unit vector, $Y=\sum_k\xi_kS_k$. Then:
--
--   1. there are unit vectors $\mathbf n,\mathbf m\in\mathbb R^3$ and $\alpha\in[0,\pi]$ with
--   $$
--   Y=(\cos\alpha)(\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf S)+i(\sin\alpha)(\mathbf m\cdot\mathbf S)\qquad(18);
--   $$
--   2. for the same $\alpha$ there are $\boldsymbol\theta\in\mathbb R^3$ and $\beta\in\mathbb R$ such that, with $U=\exp(i\,\boldsymbol\theta\cdot\mathbf S)$,
--   $$
--   UYU^\dagger=Z=(\cos\alpha)S_3+i(\sin\alpha)\big((\cos\beta)S_3+(\sin\beta)S_1\big)\qquad(19).
--   $$
--
--   This reduces the norm of $YY^\dagger$ for all unit $\xi$ to a two-parameter family.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf S=\sum_kn_kS_k$; `NormedSpace.exp` is the matrix exponential. Unit vectors of $\mathbb R^3$ are written $\sum_kn_k^2=1$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: rotating Y by a natural unitary representation of O(3) → U Y U† with U = exp(i Σ_k θ_k S_k) for some θ ∈ ℝ³; reason: the representation in question is the spin-1 representation U_1 of (7), generated by the matrices (13)
-- source:
--   Alicki, Isotropic quantum spin channels and additivity questions, arXiv:quant-ph/0402080v1, eqs. (18)–(19), p.2

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_WHAdditivity_Renyi_Model
import Definitions.Def_IsotropicSpin_spinOne
import Definitions.Def_IsotropicSpin_yMat
import Definitions.Def_IsotropicSpin_zMat

open scoped Matrix

namespace IsotropicSpin

/-- **Polar form of `Y` and its rotation to `Z`**, Alicki, arXiv:quant-ph/0402080v1, eqs. (18)–(19),
p. 2. For every unit vector `ξ ∈ ℂ³`:

1. `Y = Σ_k ξ_k S_k = (cos α)(n·S) + i (sin α)(m·S)` for two unit vectors `n, m ∈ ℝ³` and some
   `α ∈ [0, π]` (18);
2. rotating `Y` by the spin-1 representation, `U = exp(i θ·S)` for some `θ ∈ ℝ³`, turns it into
   `Z = (cos α) S₃ + i (sin α)((cos β) S₃ + (sin β) S₁)` (19), with the same `α` and some real `β`.

**Formalization Note** The page's "natural unitary representation of O(3)" is read as the spin-1
representation `θ ↦ exp(i Σ_k θ_k S_k)` generated by (13); `NormedSpace.exp` is the matrix
exponential. Unit vectors of `ℝ³` are written `Σ_k n_k² = 1`. -/
theorem eq_18_19 (ξ : Fin 3 → ℂ) (hξ : WHAdditivity.Renyi.IsUnitVector ξ) :
    ∃ (n m : Fin 3 → ℝ) (α : ℝ), ∑ k, n k ^ 2 = 1 ∧ ∑ k, m k ^ 2 = 1 ∧ α ∈ Set.Icc 0 Real.pi ∧
      yMat ξ = (Real.cos α : ℂ) • (∑ k, (n k : ℂ) • spinOne k) +
        (Complex.I * Real.sin α) • (∑ k, (m k : ℂ) • spinOne k) ∧
      ∃ (θ : Fin 3 → ℝ) (β : ℝ),
        NormedSpace.exp (Complex.I • ∑ k, (θ k : ℂ) • spinOne k) * yMat ξ *
            (NormedSpace.exp (Complex.I • ∑ k, (θ k : ℂ) • spinOne k))ᴴ = zMat α β := by sorry

end IsotropicSpin
